      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Front matter",
      "pageStart": 1,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-front-matter.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Prevention of HIV in\ninfants and young children\nA major public health problem\nHIV among children is a growing problem, particularly in the countries hardest hit by the\nAIDS epidemic. The overwhelming majority of infected children acquire the infection through\nmother-to-child transmission. Prevention of HIV infection in infants and young children is\nnow a high priority and has been the rallying point for enhanced prevention efforts.\nWhile HIV infection among infants\nis a problem all over the world, it is\nmost acute in sub-Saharan Africa,\nwhere almost 90% of all HIV-\ninfected children live.\nHIV/",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens a 2002 WHO information sheet on preventing HIV in infants and young children, describing mother-to-child transmission as the main route of infection and sub-Saharan Africa as the worst-affected region. It explains the timing and risk of transmission during pregnancy, delivery and breastfeeding and gives UNAIDS/WHO figures on children infected and dying in 2001.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-000",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A public health solution that works",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-000-a-public-health-solution-that-works.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The rate of transmission of HIV from infected pregnant women to their infants has decreased to\nless than 2% in industrialized countries through the use of highly effective antiretroviral drug\nregimens for the prevention of perinatal transmission (or for treating the mother) combined\nwith elective Caesarean section and replacement feeding from birth. Some other countries,\nsuch as Thailand, have also succeeded in reducing the number of children infected with HIV.\nAchieving similar results in other developing countries, some of which are severely affected by\nthe AIDS epidemic, will require addres",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that mother-to-child HIV transmission has fallen below 2% in industrialised countries through antiretroviral drugs, elective Caesarean section and replacement feeding. It cites Thailand's fall in paediatric AIDS cases since prevention programmes began in 1997, alongside falling HIV prevalence among pregnant women.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The UNGASS Declaration of Commitment",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-the-ungass-declaration-of-commitment.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In June 2001, during the United Nations General Assembly Special Session (UNGASS) on\nHIV/AIDS, 189 Member States declared their commitment to take action to:\n\"By 2005, reduce the proportion of infants infected with HIV by 20 per cent, and by 50 per\ncent by 2010 … \"\n1,400\n1,400\n1,200\n1,200\n1,000\n1,000\n800\n800\n600\n600\n400\n400\n200\n200\n0\n3.5%\n3.0%\n2.5%\n2.0%\n1.5%\n1.0%\n0.5%\n0%\n0%\nProgramme for the prevention\n mother-to-child HIV transmission\n1990 1991 1992 1993 1994 1995 1996 1997 1998 1999 2000 2001\nDecline in paediatric AIDS cases in Thailand\nNumber of paediatric AIDS cases\nSeroprevalence in women",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Quotes the 2001 UNGASS commitment to cut the share of infants infected with HIV by 20% by 2005 and 50% by 2010, with a chart of Thailand's declining paediatric AIDS cases. It begins listing needed interventions, starting with primary prevention of HIV among women, including during pregnancy and breastfeeding.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "• Prevention of unintended pregnancies among HIV-infected women. Most HIV-",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-prevention-of-unintended-pregnancies-among-hiv-i.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "infected women in the developing world are unaware of their serostatus. Family planning\nservices need to be strengthened so that all women, including those who are infected, can\nreceive support and services to prevent unintended pregnancies. Women known to be\ninfected with HIV should receive essential care and support services, including family\nplanning and other reproductive health services, so that they can make informed decisions\nabout their future reproductive lives.\n• Prevention of HIV transmission from HIV-infected women to their infants.\nA package of specific interventions has been iden",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the list of interventions, calling for stronger family planning to prevent unintended pregnancies among women with HIV, most of whom do not know their status. It describes the package to prevent transmission to infants, including antiretrovirals, safer delivery, infant-feeding counselling and HIV testing, and stresses care and support for mothers and families.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A framework for action to prevent HIV infection in infants and young children",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-a-framework-for-action-to-prevent-hiv-infection.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "All women \n prevent HIV infection\nHIV-infected women \n prevent unintended pregnancy\nHIV-infected women \n prevent transmission to infants and young children\nHIV-infected women, their infant and family \n provide care and support",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A framework diagram showing four levels of action: preventing HIV in all women, preventing unintended pregnancy and transmission to infants among women with HIV, and providing care and support.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A comprehensive approach to prevent HIV infection in infants and young children",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-a-comprehensive-approach-to-prevent-hiv-infectio.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "infants and young children\nBased on WHO estimates, the percentage\nof women receiving antenatal care, defined\nas at least one visit, ranges from 20% to\n99% in Africa, with an average of 62%.\nThe percentage of women having a pro-\nfessionally attended delivery ranges from\n2% to 99%, with an average of 36%.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives WHO estimates for Africa showing antenatal care coverage averaging 62% and professionally attended deliveries averaging 36%.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Specific interventions to reduce mother-to-child transmission of HIV",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-specific-interventions-to-reduce-mother-to-child.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Antiretroviral drug use. A number of antiretroviral drug regimens – based on zidovudine,\nzidovudine and lamivudine or nevirapine, or combinations used in highly active antiretroviral\ntherapy (HAART) – have been shown to be effective in reducing mother-to-child\ntransmission of HIV. The choice of antiretroviral regimen should be made locally, taking\ninto account issues of feasibility, efficacy and cost.\nSafer delivery practices. It has been shown that elective Caesarean section can help to reduce\nthe risk of mother-to-child HIV transmission. This, however, may not be an appropriate\nintervention",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes specific interventions to reduce mother-to-child HIV transmission, including antiretroviral regimens chosen locally for feasibility, efficacy and cost, and safer delivery practices that limit invasive procedures. It gives infant-feeding advice: avoiding breastfeeding where replacement feeding is acceptable, feasible, affordable, sustainable and safe, and otherwise exclusive breastfeeding in the first months.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "More than the cost of drugs alone",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-more-than-the-cost-of-drugs-alone.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "For many years now, the cost of antiretroviral drugs has been seen as the key hurdle to\nimplementing interventions that can prevent HIV infection in infants and young children\nin resource-constrained countries. Now, with the negotiation of more advantageous pricing\nagreements , as well as large-scale donations of some drugs, the possibility of pregnant\nwomen in these countries having access to antiretroviral drugs has increased enormously.\nEven with the cost barrier removed, many hurdles to implementing interventions remain.\nThe most important may be the inability of health systems in some of",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Argues that, although antiretroviral drug prices have fallen and donations have expanded access, weak health systems remain the main obstacle. Limited and late use of antenatal care and lack of HIV testing services still hinder delivery of prevention interventions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What WHO is doing",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-what-who-is-doing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The task of helping countries prepare to engage in this effort is enormous, and WHO is\ncommitted to working with its traditional partners – ministries of health, international\nagencies, nongovernmental organizations and people living with HIV/AIDS – to make the\nprevention of HIV in infants a reality, especially in the hardest hit developing countries.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States WHO's commitment to work with ministries, agencies, NGOs and people living with HIV to prevent HIV in infants, especially in the hardest-hit countries.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Highlighting the importance of prevention",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-highlighting-the-importance-of-prevention.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO is committed to keeping the focus on the best and most humane ways to prevent\ntransmission of HIV to infants and young children – that is, keeping young women free from\ninfection. HIV prevention, in general, is a key focus for WHO’s Department of HIV/AIDS.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that WHO will keep its focus on preventing HIV among young women as the best way to protect infants.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Including HIV in family planning programmes",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-including-hiv-in-family-planning-programmes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Through the efforts of its Department of Reproductive Health and Research, WHO will\nbuild on its long history of supporting countries to improve the quality and coverage of their\nfamily planning and other reproductive health services. It will also continue to advocate for\na shift in emphasis in family planning programmes, with greater attention being given to\nsimultaneously preventing infection and pregnancy (dual protection). In some cases, this\nwill entail greater emphasis being placed on the use of condoms, both male and female,\nas a primary family planning method.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes WHO's support for family planning programmes, including promoting dual protection against infection and pregnancy and greater use of male and female condoms.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Strengthening the infrastructure for preventing HIV infection in infants",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-strengthening-the-infrastructure-for-preventing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Expanding antenatal care services. If preventing HIV infection in infants and young\nchildren is to be successful, women must have expanded access to antenatal care and\nmust use the services more frequently and earlier in pregnancy than is currently the case.\nTo address this issue, WHO will support maternal and reproductive health programmes in\ncountries through its Making Pregnancy Safer Initiative.\nIncreasing access to voluntary counselling and testing. Even if women use antenatal\ncare services, they must have access to voluntary counselling and testing to detect any\nHIV infection and must be",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines WHO's work to strengthen infrastructure for preventing HIV in infants, including expanding antenatal care through the Making Pregnancy Safer initiative and increasing access to voluntary counselling and testing. It stresses integrating prevention of infant HIV into national health systems.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Increasing access to HIV care",
      "pageStart": 5,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-increasing-access-to-hiv-care.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO is intensifying efforts to increase access to HIV care in resource-limited settings, while\nstrengthening linkages between prevention and care activities – to maximize the impact of both.\nProgrammes for the prevention of HIV in infants and young children are identifying large numbers\nof women infected with HIV. WHO is developing guidance on key elements of a comprehen-\nsive approach to care, treatment and support for these women, their infants and their families.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that WHO is expanding access to HIV care in resource-limited settings and developing guidance on care, treatment and support for women identified through prevention programmes and their families.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Providing guidance",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-providing-guidance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Keeping abreast of the science. WHO actively reviews developments in the science\nthat underlies the prevention of HIV infection in infants and young children, assessing the\nstrength of evidence and highlighting key gaps in the research base. This is an important\nservice to countries, many of which do not have timely access to complete information on\nscientific developments and are thus at a disadvantage in developing and improving\nprevention policies and programmes.\nChoice and use of antiretroviral drugs. WHO is reviewing the evidence and developing\nguidance that countries need to enable them",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes WHO's guidance role, including reviewing the science, advising on antiretroviral regimens for preventing transmission and treating HIV, and supporting safe infant feeding. It also mentions help for women with HIV to avoid unintended pregnancy and models to show where prevention investments are most cost-effective.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67344/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Technical support to countries for a public health approach to prevention and",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-technical-support-to-countries-for-a-public-heal.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "care. WHO is committed to helping countries deliver both the best HIV prevention and the\nbest HIV/AIDS-related care. In doing so, WHO hopes to elaborate the links and mutual\nbenefits between prevention and care and to develop and strengthen a public health\napproach to confronting the AIDS epidemic in the future.\nFor more information, contact: WORLD HEALTH ORGANIZATION - Family and Community Health Cluster - Department of HIV/AIDS\n20, avenue Appia - CH-1211 Geneva 27 - Switzerland - E-mail: hiv-aids@who.int\n© World Health Organization  2002\nAll rights reserved\nThe World Health Organization does",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States WHO's commitment to support a public health approach linking HIV prevention and care, followed by contact details for the WHO Department of HIV/AIDS and 2002 copyright.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    }
  ]
